Feature: Filter list by tag

  `yx list --tag <name>` filters the list to only show yaks that have
  the given tag. The --tag flag is repeatable and can contain
  comma-separated values.

  Rule: Single tag filter

    `--tag foo` shows only yaks tagged `foo`, hiding untagged yaks
    and yaks with different tags.

    Example: Shows yak with matching tag, hides others

      Given a yak "deploy" tagged "epic"
      And a yak "fix bug" tagged "urgent"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should see "deploy"
      But I should not see "fix bug"

    Example: Hides untagged yaks

      Given a yak "cleanup" with no tags
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should not see "cleanup"

  Rule: OR within a clause

    Comma-separated tags within a single --tag flag are ORed.
    `--tag foo,bar` shows yaks that have `foo` OR `bar` (or both).

    Example: Comma-separated tags match yaks with either tag

      Given a yak "A" tagged "blocked"
      And a yak "B" tagged "need-design"
      And a yak "C" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag blocked,need-design
      Then I should see "A"
      And I should see "B"
      But I should not see "C"

    Example: Yak with both OR tags also matches

      Given a yak "D" tagged "blocked" and "need-design"
      When I list yaks with --tag blocked,need-design
      Then I should see "D"

  Rule: AND across clauses

    Multiple --tag flags require a yak to satisfy ALL of them.
    `--tag foo --tag bar` means the yak must have both tags.

    Example: Multiple --tag flags require yak to have all

      Given a yak "A" tagged "epic" and "blocked"
      And a yak "B" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic --tag blocked
      Then I should see "A"
      But I should not see "B"

    Example: Yak missing one required tag is hidden

      Given a yak "C" tagged "blocked"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic --tag blocked
      Then I should not see "C"

  Rule: Combined AND/OR

    Clauses combine: each --tag is an AND clause, commas within
    are OR. `--tag a,b --tag c` means must have `c` AND must
    have either `a` or `b`.

    Example: --tag blocked,need-design --tag epic requires epic AND (blocked OR need-design)

      Given a yak "A" tagged "epic" and "blocked"
      And a yak "B" tagged "epic" and "need-design"
      And a yak "C" tagged "epic"
      And a yak "D" tagged "blocked"
      When I list yaks with --tag blocked,need-design --tag epic
      Then I should see "A"
      And I should see "B"
      But I should not see "C"
      And I should not see "D"

  Rule: Ancestor context

    Only yaks that match the tag filter are "hits". Ancestors of
    hits are shown to preserve tree structure. Children of hits
    are NOT shown unless they also match independently.

    Example: Non-matching parent shown when child matches

      Given a yak "infrastructure" with no tags
      And a yak "deploy" under "infrastructure" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should see "infrastructure"
      And I should see "deploy"

    Example: Child of matching parent hidden if child doesn't match

      Given a yak "infrastructure" tagged "epic"
      And a yak "cleanup" under "infrastructure" with no tags
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should see "infrastructure"
      But I should not see "cleanup"

    Example: Both shown when both match independently

      Given a yak "infrastructure" tagged "epic"
      And a yak "deploy" under "infrastructure" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should see "infrastructure"
      And I should see "deploy"

    Example: Full ancestor chain shown for deeply nested match

      Given a yak "grandparent" with no tags
      And a yak "parent" under "grandparent" with no tags
      And a yak "child" under "parent" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then I should see "grandparent"
      And I should see "parent"
      And I should see "child"

  Rule: Combines with --only

    `--tag` and `--only` are ANDed. A yak must satisfy both
    filters to be a "hit". Ancestor context still applies.

    Example: --tag epic --only done shows only done yaks with epic tag

      Given a yak "A" tagged "epic" with state "done"
      And a yak "B" tagged "epic" with state "todo"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic --only done
      Then I should see "A"
      But I should not see "B"

    Example: Ancestor context still applies with combined filters

      Given a yak "parent" with state "todo" and no tags
      And a yak "child" under "parent" tagged "epic" with state "done"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic --only done
      Then I should see "parent"
      And I should see "child"

  Rule: Tag normalization

    The @ prefix is stripped from --tag values, same as yx tag add.

    Example: --tag @epic treated same as --tag epic

      Given a yak "A" tagged "epic"
      When I list yaks with --tag @epic
      Then I should see "A"

    Example: --tag @foo,@bar strips @ from all values

      Given a yak "A" tagged "foo"
      When I list yaks with --tag @foo,@bar
      Then I should see "A"

  Rule: No matches

    When no yaks match the tag filter, the result is empty.

    Example: Empty result when no yaks have the tag

      Given a yak "A" tagged "urgent"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic
      Then the output should be empty

    Example: JSON format returns empty array

      Given a yak "A" tagged "urgent"
      When I list yaks with --tag epic --format json
      Then the output should be "[]"
